      Currently in active development against a live documentary-style project. Specific client and footage details aren't public. Captions are still being human-proofread before anything is called final. Nothing from it has shipped yet. I'd rather say that plainly than oversell a system that's still being built.
    </div>

    <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:36px 0 10px;">One project, every shape</p>
    <div class="format-row">
      <div class="format-item"><div class="format-frame v"><span>9:16</span></div><p class="format-caption">Vertical cut for social and mobile</p></div>
      <div class="format-item"><div class="format-frame h"><span>16:9</span></div><p class="format-caption">Widescreen master</p></div>
      <div class="format-item"><div class="format-frame h"><span>TRT</span></div><p class="format-caption">Full-runtime cut, broadcast length</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="prose" style="margin-top:20px;">Same source footage, same story, three deliverable shapes, built from one timeline instead of three separate edits.</p>

    <div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/about/pete-on-set-shear.jpg" alt="Pete Guzzo on set" /></div>
    <p class="img-caption">Still the one in the chair</p>

    <p class="prose" style="margin-top:32px;">Can it do the whole edit? It gets pretty close. I'm not going to lie, it's a pretty damn good edit. Is it exactly what I want, as the editor and as the director? No. Because I'm still the only one who can do that. I still have to go in there. I still have to tell the Verse and the whole team inside it exactly what I'm looking for, and that's how it actually gets made. That's still storytelling. You're not taking that away from the human. You're automating the process around it so the human can actually be functional.</p>

    <p class="prose">I still know how to edit. I can open Premiere or DaVinci myself, the same way a mechanic who's automated most of a diagnostic process can still pop the hood and get his hands dirty. Cars have a lot more electronics in them than they used to, sure, but the fundamentals are still the fundamentals. Same here. I just don't have the time anymore, because my one job turned into five.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="bg-grey bordered"><div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">Why it exists, and how it actually runs.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Long-form documentary, reality, interview, and social work carries a huge mechanical burden before a single creative decision gets made: organizing hours of footage, syncing separate audio, transcribing, finding the one quote, finishing in multiple formats. This removes that burden so the decisions happen faster, not instead of happening.</p>

    <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:10px;">The mission order</p>
    <div class="mission-steps">
      <span class="mission-step">Intake</span><span class="mission-step">Protect</span><span class="mission-step">Inventory</span><span class="mission-step">Sync</span><span class="mission-step">Transcribe</span><span class="mission-step">Map</span><span class="mission-step">Organize</span><span class="mission-step">Discover</span><span class="mission-step">Build</span><span class="mission-step">Finish</span><span class="mission-step">Verify</span><span class="mission-step mission-step--gate">Human review</span><span class="mission-step">Revise</span><span class="mission-step mission-step--gate">Deliver</span><span class="mission-step">Learn</span>
    </div>

    <p class="prose" style="margin-top:32px;">Every stage hands off a versioned output plus a receipt: source, assumptions, sync evidence, restoration and mix notes. A picture revision has to match its graphics and audio receipts before QC passes it. Originals never get touched. Material gets tagged by how real it is: a spontaneous moment, a producer-fed pickup, a staged setup. Nothing is presented as more candid than it actually was. Nothing invents a quote, manufactures a timeline, or cuts context to make a clip hit harder.</p>

    <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:36px 0 10px;">How it actually checks its own work</p>
    <p class="prose">Footage becomes a searchable index before anyone edits a frame. Every shot gets tagged by mood, person, and quality rating. Every spoken line gets mapped back to its source, timecode, and sync confidence. Story choices get made from that index, not from whatever happened to be handy.</p>
    <p class="prose">Every picture version carries a revision ID generated from its own contents. Its graphics, audio mix, and QC receipts have to carry that same ID or the delivery gets rejected as stale. A new aspect ratio, a new grade, a new music swap, each gets its own ID. Older versions get archived, never overwritten, so nothing is a one-way door.</p>
    <p class="prose">QC runs on measurement, not a gut check: frame counts and audio length checked against picture, loudness and true-peak measured on the final encode, automated checks that titles and captions never collide with a face, a shake/blur score on every cutaway that flags anything too rough to use. Camera-to-recorder sync gets verified at the start, middle, and end of a take, not assumed. And the check is never done by the same agent that built the thing being checked.</p>

    <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:36px 0 10px;">What stays human, full stop</p>
    <ul class="gate-list">
      <li>Final story meaning</li>
      <li>Sensitive edits</li>
      <li>Final creative selection</li>
      <li>Any material claim</li>
      <li>Rights exceptions</li>
      <li>Irreversible changes</li>
      <li>Publishing, and spending</li>
    </ul>

    <blockquote class="pull">"The target is not an automated video. The target is a human editor with extraordinary leverage."</blockquote>

    <p class="prose" style="margin-top:28px;">What it's built toward, not what's shipped yet, is a full workflow application with its own modules for source registry, sync, transcripts, a searchable bite bank, B-roll retrieval, native Premiere and Resolve support, review and approvals, QC evidence. Export packages for Premiere and Resolve exist and validate offline. A live bridge into the edit applications exists too, but the first real import through it isn't verified working yet. That part's still in development, and I'd rather say so than claim it's finished.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="bordered"><div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">Inside the Verse.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Not the footage. None of that's public, and won't be. The process.</p>
    <p class="prose">Most of what exists right now, footage indexes, paper edits, QC sheets, overlay graphics, carries identifiable client and guest information, so none of it is going on this page. What does get shown here is held to one standard: no faces, no names, no story IDs, ever. A real before/after spectrogram from a dialogue-restoration pass exists and meets that bar; it's queued for this page once the last review pass on it is done.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section id="barstool" class="bg-grey bordered"><div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Proof, not a pitch deck</p>
    <h2 class="display">One contract, start to finish: Barstool Sports.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Closed, built, and still running. One account that shows the production side and the AI side working together, not as separate projects.</p>

    <p class="prose">I closed this one myself, on the strength of the studio. It's a recurring weekly live broadcast client, not a one-off. Barstool Sports runs out of Shear Media Studios every week. Five cameras, up to ten when we need it, shoot live in 4K from St. Petersburg and go out over SRT to their control room in Chicago in real time. Two additional lines sit in reserve for redundancy, plus a return SRT path back to our floor. Chicago records its own ISOs locally while doing the live line cut. The show gets produced there and built here.</p>

    <div class="status-note">
      <strong>Live, under pressure</strong>
      Getting that signal path online wasn't simple. Our building's fiber runs between two structures with no static IP addresses available. That's a real constraint, not a design choice. I'm not a network engineer, but I worked out an initial routing solution myself. It still hit a wall. From there I worked with Codex in real time, live, on a call with Barstool's engineers in Vegas and Chicago, diagnosing the block and getting it fixed while the call was happening, not after. All five primary SRT lines, the two safety lines, and the return path came online, live, with everyone watching. That's the AI side at its most literal. Not a post-production tool. A second set of hands on a live production problem, while I stayed the one making the calls.
    </div>

    <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:28px 0 10px;">The signal path</p>
