    </div>

    <div class="decks" style="margin-top:28px;">
      <div class="deck">
        <p class="tag">Live now</p>
        <h3>Shea 1.0</h3>
        <p>Deployed and embedded on the live site. Registration-gated chat behind four independent rate limits: per visitor, site-wide, per IP, and registrations per IP. Not just one usage cap. Every brief it generates goes out through a message queue with its own dead-letter queue, so a failed email doesn't just vanish silently. Covers real studio spaces and a handful of pricing and add-on rules. Every conversation ends in a brief routed to a human. Nothing closes without one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="deck">
        <p class="tag">In development</p>
        <h3>Shea 2.0</h3>
        <p>Staged, not live. On its own Worker, its own data store, its own queues. Zero shared state with production, by design. A confirm-draft flow, an inline comparison panel, a budget-entry tool, and a smarter studio-fit conversation, landing one feature at a time, each its own gated commit, never a big-bang merge to the real site.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cta-section" style="margin-top:36px;">
      <a href="https://shearmediastudios.com" class="btn">Talk to Shea, live →</a>
    </div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="bordered"><div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Inside CORTEX</p>
    <h2 class="display">One idea, start to finish.</h2>
    <p class="lede">Shea and the Verse aren't two separate systems. They're the front half and the back half of the same chain.</p>
    <p class="prose">A client brings an idea to Shea. Shea turns it into a brief a human can actually act on. A crew shoots it. From there the Verse takes over: organize, sync, transcribe, cut, finish, in three screen shapes, every version landing in the Review Desk before it goes anywhere. Same chain, same rule the whole way through. Nothing skips a step. Nothing skips me.</p>

